from: optimizer-pd-role-ceiling
session: 4th design resolution — achieved-so-far numerator, found post-commit-1

## What changed

Commit `a694012a` (the joint-allocation fix, planner-approved 2026-07-14) landed with a
still-subtle bug in the `achievedSoFar` numerator, found while designing the "FairShare-pressure
invariant" test (§ Test plan item 6) — no test in `a694012a` exercised genuine multi-model
fair-share competition (mean>0), so it went uncaught until then.

**The bug:** `a694012a` read `originalRC_role`/the achieved-so-far numerator from
`pickerState[satIdx][role]` at function entry. That's a faithful proxy for "how much has this
role already achieved, this call" only when nothing external interferes with `pickerState`
between calls. `GreedyByScoreOptimizer.allocateForModel` violates that on every outer call after
the first: it rebuilds `pickerState` fresh from `RequiredCapacity` and externally caps it to that
iteration's fair-share budget *before* calling into `allocateForModelPaired`. Whenever more than
one model competes for the same GPU pool, that cap sits strictly below the true remaining demand
— and reading achieved-so-far off the capped value misread the unfunded gap as "already
achieved," inflating a role's progress and letting a sibling role over-commit past its true joint
ceiling.

**The fix:** committed capacity is now computed directly from the replica delta already tracked
in `targets[]`/`stateMap` (`targets[v] - CurrentReplicas`, × `PerReplicaCapacity`, summed over the
role's variants) instead of from `pickerState`. This is exact regardless of how many outer
fair-share calls have run or how tightly any of them capped their budget, because `targets[]`
persists and accumulates across the whole optimizer pass. `RequiredCapacity` itself (the
denominator) is now read as a fixed value off the analyzer's `RoleCapacities` (via the
`saturationRoleView` helper already added in `a694012a`) rather than off `pickerState`, for the
same reason.

Committed as `911e13b7` (analyzer_helpers.go fix + the regression test that caught it) and
`4fdd1123` (two more test-plan scenarios: multi-variant-per-role, synthetic 3-role — no code
changes, unaffected by the bug).

## Update the plan doc

`planning/optimizer-pd-role-ceiling-plan.md` § Design — corrected algorithm currently documents
3 "Resolved during implementation" points (snapshot location, `denom_role` derivation, the
`achievedSoFar` clamp). Please add a 4th:

> **4. `originalRC_role`/achieved-so-far numerator must come from actual commits, not
> pickerState.** Resolution #1 above (snapshot `pickerState[satIdx][role]` at function entry)
> is only correct for the *denominator* derivation, not for the achieved-so-far numerator.
> `GreedyByScoreOptimizer`'s per-iteration fair-share budget cap on `pickerState` means the
> at-entry snapshot can sit below the true remaining demand whenever more than one model is
> active — misreading the unfunded gap as already-achieved progress. Fixed by computing
> committed capacity from `targets[v] - stateMap[v].CurrentReplicas` (summed per role, across
> that role's variants) instead — this is exact across any number of outer fair-share calls
> since `targets[]` persists across the whole pass. No test in the original 3-resolution round
> exercised genuine multi-model competition (mean>0 in `fairShareScaleUp`), which is why this
> surfaced only once the FairShare-pressure invariant test (§ Test plan item 6) was designed.

This also means the formula block just above (the `achievedSoFar_role = ... / denom_role`
line) should read `committed_role` (computed from `targets`/`stateMap`) instead of
`(originalRC_role − pickerState[i][role])` — the pickerState-based expression is what was wrong.

## Open questions / follow-ups

None — this is a closed loop (bug found by the coder's own test design, fixed, tested, committed;
full reasoning trace is in the two commit messages). Flagging for the plan doc only so it stays
the authoritative record rather than this handoff, per the coder's own preference stated in the
prior handoff on this topic.
